    "## Functional dependency\n",
    "The functional dependency that we will focus on is\n",
    "- album, year -> genre\n",
    "\n",
    "The function below determines all violations of this functional dependency. Remember that this functional dependency means that album and year should determine the value for genre, i.e., for every two rows with the same value of album and year, the value of genre should also be the same.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Violations in our example table\n",
    "Notice that there are multiple violations in the table for this functional dependency. Also notice that the song \"Beat It\" is involved in two violations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1f35c5c6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def fd_violations(df):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Determines all violations of the functional dependency album, year -> genre\n",
    "\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        df: DataFrame to check for violations\n",
    "\n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        List of violation pairs (row indices)\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    violations = []\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Loop over all pairs of rows\n",
    "    for i in range(len(df)):\n",
    "        for j in range(i + 1, len(df)):\n",
    "            row1 = df.iloc[i]\n",
    "            row2 = df.iloc[j]\n",
    "\n",
    "            # Check if album and year are the same\n",
    "            if row1['album'] == row2['album'] and row1['year'] == row2['year']:\n",
    "                # Check if genre is different (violation)\n",
    "                if row1['genre'] != row2['genre']:\n",
    "                    violations.append((i, j))\n",
    "\n",
    "    return violations\n",
    "\n",
    "def display_violation(df, row1_idx, row2_idx, violation_number=None):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Displays the details of a functional dependency violation between two rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        df: DataFrame containing the data\n",
    "        row1_idx: Index of the first row in violation\n",
    "        row2_idx: Index of the second row in violation\n",
    "        violation_number: Optional violation number for display\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    row1 = df.iloc[row1_idx]\n",
    "    row2 = df.iloc[row2_idx]\n",
    "\n",
    "    if violation_number:\n",
    "        print(f\"Violation {violation_number}:\")\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        print(f\"Violation (rows {row1_idx} and {row2_idx}):\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    print(f\"  Row {row1_idx}: {row1['song_title']} - Album: {row1['album']}, Year: {row1['year']}, Genre: {row1['genre']}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  Row {row2_idx}: {row2['song_title']} - Album: {row2['album']}, Year: {row2['year']}, Genre: {row2['genre']}\")\n",
    "    print()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Execute the function and display results\n",
    "violations = fd_violations(data)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Functional dependency violations for 'album, year -> genre':\")\n",
    "print(f\"Number of violations found: {len(violations)}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "if violations:\n",
    "    print(\"\\nViolation details:\")\n",
    "    for i, (row1_idx, row2_idx) in enumerate(violations):\n",
    "        display_violation(data, row1_idx, row2_idx, i+1)\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(\"No violations found - the functional dependency holds for this dataset.\")"
   ]
