    "## Generating possible repairs\n",
    "\n",
    "When a functional dependency violation is detected between two rows, there are exactly four possible ways to repair the violation for the FD **album, year → genre**:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Delete first row** - Remove the first conflicting row entirely from the dataset\n",
    "2. **Delete second row** - Remove the second conflicting row entirely from the dataset  \n",
    "3. **Update first row's genre** - Change the genre of the first row to match the second row's genre\n",
    "4. **Update second row's genre** - Change the genre of the second row to match the first row's genre\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why these repairs work:\n",
    "- **Deletion repairs** eliminate one of the conflicting rows, so no violation remains\n",
    "- **Update repairs** make both rows have the same genre value for the same album+year combination, satisfying the functional dependency\n",
    "\n",
    "Each repair has different implications:\n",
    "- Deletions result in data loss but guarantee no further conflicts for that pair\n",
    "- Updates preserve all data but may introduce new conflicts if the updated genre conflicts with other rows sharing the same album+year combination\n",
    "\n",
    "### EXERCISE 1: Implement repair four\n",
    "The code below only implements the first three repair options. The fourth repair is left as an exercise. Write the code for it analogous to the third repair."
   ]
