    "\n",
    "## Optional Exercise 1: Alternative Violation Scenario\n",
    "\n",
    "### Background\n",
    "In the examples above, we assumed that violations occur because a song has the wrong genre assigned. However, there's another possible accident scenario: **a song might have the wrong album and year combination**, while the genre is actually correct.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Exercise Description\n",
    "Consider this alternative violation scenario:\n",
    "- A song is correctly categorized by genre\n",
    "- But due to data entry error, the song has been assigned to the wrong album and year\n",
    "- The correct album+year combination exists elsewhere in the dataset for songs of the same genre\n",
    "\n",
    "### Your Task\n",
    "Implement an alternative repair strategy that handles this scenario:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Modify the repair generation** to include \"album+year correction\" repairs\n",
    "- Update the album+year of one song to match another song **with the same genre**\n",
    "- Only use album+year combinations that already exist in the dataset (closed world assumption)\n",
    "2. **Modify the repair application**\n",
    "3. **Cost consideration**: Updating album+year should cost more than updating genre alone (suggest cost = 2)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example\n",
    "If \"Beat It\" (Rock genre) has album \"Thriller\", year 1982, but violates the FD, you could repair it by updating its album+year to match \"Bohemian Rhapsody\" (Rock genre) → album \"A Night at the Opera\", year 1975.\n",
    "\n",
    "Try implementing this extended repair strategy and test it on the example dataset!\n",
    "\n",
    "## Optional Exercise 2: Consistent Query Answering (CQA)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Background\n",
    "\n",
    "When a database contains violations of integrity constraints (like functional dependencies), different repair strategies can lead to different \"correct\" versions of the data. **Consistent Query Answering (CQA)** is a principled approach to querying inconsistent databases that returns only answers that would be consistently returned across **all possible valid repairs**.\n",
    "\n",
    "The key idea behind CQA is:\n",
    "- Given an inconsistent database and a query\n",
    "- Generate all possible valid repairs of the database\n",
    "- Execute the query on each repaired database\n",
    "- Return only the results that appear in **every single** repaired database\n",
    "\n",
    "This intersection of results represents the **certain answers** - data that we can be confident about regardless of which specific repair was applied.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why is CQA Important?\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Uncertainty handling**: When data quality issues exist, CQA provides a conservative but reliable approach\n",
    "2. **Avoiding arbitrary choices**: Rather than picking one specific repair, CQA considers all reasonable repairs\n",
    "3. **Decision support**: In critical applications, knowing what is \"certainly true\" is more valuable than potentially incorrect specific answers\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example Intuition\n",
    "\n",
    "If we have violations in our music database:\n",
    "- Some repairs might delete \"Beat It\" by Michael Jackson\n",
    "- Other repairs might keep \"Beat It\" but change its genre\n",
    "- A query asking for \"all artists\" would return different results depending on the repair\n",
    "- CQA would only return artists that appear in **every possible repair**\n",
    "\n",
    "This ensures we never return information that might be wrong due to data quality issues.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Exercise description\n",
    "\n",
    "The exercise is to\n",
    "- Program two functions for two queries: one is two get all distinct artists in the table, and the other is all distinct energy levels of the Michael Jackson songs.\n",
    "- Then program a function that can produce the CQA result by running the query on all valid possibl repaired tables and produce the intersection of those results. These are the results that are consistently produced in all possible repairs.\n",
    "- Before applying the function to the example scenario, first determine in your head what the CQA answer should be (this is something you would need to be able to do on the exam as well).\n",
    "- Then apply the function and check whether your theoretically determined answer is correct.\n",
    "\n",
    "There are of course more efficient algorithms for computing the CQA answer, but by staying so close to the intended meaning of CQA, you can be sure that the answer is correct here. Our scenario is small enough for an exhaustive brute-force approach.\n",
